About this fork: LuaJIT under MemoryDenyWriteExecute
----------------------------------------------------

This is a fork of LuaJIT v2.1 whose JIT compiler keeps working when Linux
refuses to make writable memory executable: the kernel's PR_SET_MDWE and
systemd's MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes. Upstream LuaJIT panics there with
"runtime code generation failed, restricted kernel?" as soon as it compiles
a trace, and its FFI callbacks crash even with -joff.

The fork backs machine code with a sealed memfd and switches each code area
between a shared read-write view and a private read-execute view with
mmap(MAP_FIXED). No mapping is ever writable and executable, and no mprotect()
call adds PROT_EXEC, so the W^X guarantee is preserved, not bypassed. fork()
is handled by write-sealing the file and moving each process to a new one
before its next write.

Enable it at runtime (Linux, any build of this fork; default off):

 luajit -Omcoderemap=1 app.lua
 jit.opt.start("mcoderemap=1")   -- from Lua or an embedding host

Set it before the first trace is compiled, or call jit.flush() afterwards.
Building with -DLUAJIT_SECURITY_MCODE=2 (./build --mcode 2) makes it the
default. The flake exposes that build as packages.<system>.luajit-mdwe-remap
(Linux). Without the switch the fork behaves like upstream.

Cost, measured against upstream on x86_64 Linux: no measurable change on 19
steady-state benchmarks; about +30-45% on a compile-heavy stress test, since
each protection switch is a remap. Smaller code areas lower that cost
(-Osizemcode=16 measured +20%, versus +31% at the default 64 KB), but an
area too small for a large trace aborts that trace. bin/sizemcode-tune times
your own script at several sizes and reports the fastest.

Build and test: nix develop -c ./build and ./test (Zig build plus upstream's
Makefile). Design, evidence and measurements: docs/MDWE_SPEC.md,
docs/MDWE_RESEARCH.md; fixes also proposed upstream: docs/UPSTREAM_CANDIDATES.md.

README for LuaJIT 2.1
---------------------

LuaJIT is a Just-In-Time (JIT) compiler for the Lua programming language.

Project Homepage: https://luajit.org/

LuaJIT is Copyright (C) 2005-2026 Mike Pall.
LuaJIT is free software, released under the MIT license.
See full Copyright Notice in the COPYRIGHT file or in luajit.h.

Documentation for LuaJIT is available in HTML format.
Please point your favorite browser to:

 doc/luajit.html

